\chapter{Architecture}

\section{Concepts}

The system decomposes into three concepts, each a chapter that crosses the
whole stack (types, logic, and the seams it exposes to its neighbours):

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Accounts} --- the store of accounts and their balances. It
    owns what an account \emph{is} and the only operations that change a
    balance.
  \item \textbf{Ledger} --- transactions and postings. It owns the
    \emph{invariant}: it refuses to apply a transaction whose postings do not
    sum to zero.
  \item \textbf{Api} --- the surface. For this stage it is a small command
    line program that opens accounts, posts one transaction, and prints the
    balances.
\end{itemize}

\section{Technology}

Plain C, one static binary, no dependencies beyond libc. The domain is small
and correctness-critical, which is exactly where C's directness helps and its
footguns (floating point, partial mutation) must be designed out rather than
hoped away.

\section{Decision records}

\subsection{Decision: integer cents, never floating point}
Chosen: balances are \texttt{long} cents. Considered and rejected:
\texttt{double} dollars, because \texttt{0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3} in binary floating
point, and a ledger that cannot represent a dime exactly is not fit for
purpose.

\subsection{Decision: the invariant lives in Ledger, not in Accounts}
Chosen: \texttt{Accounts} exposes a neutral \texttt{acc\_apply(id, delta)}
that changes one balance, and \texttt{Ledger} is the only place that knows a
transaction must sum to zero. Considered and rejected: enforcing balance in
Accounts, which would force every balance change to know about transactions
and couple the two concepts permanently. Keeping the invariant in one concept
is what makes it auditable.

\subsection{Decision: transactions are atomic}
Chosen: \texttt{ledger\_post} validates the sum \emph{and} every account
before it mutates anything, so a rejected transaction leaves no partial
effect. This is why Accounts exposes an \texttt{acc\_exists} check separate
from \texttt{acc\_apply}.

\section{The dependency contract}

A concept may reference another only along an allowed edge. Anything not
listed is forbidden and fails the conformance gate. The forbidden directions
are the design: \textbf{Accounts must not know about Ledger} (the store is
ignorant of transactions), and \textbf{no concept below Api may depend on
Api} (the domain must not reach up into its surface).

% --- machine-readable policy: enforced by `ldd conformance` ---
% ARCH-ALLOW: Ledger -> Accounts
% ARCH-ALLOW: Api -> Ledger
% ARCH-ALLOW: Api -> Accounts
